  "id": "DrugBank_DB00714 - MONDO_14231",
  "verbalization": "Apomorphine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Juvenile Onset Parkinson Disease 19a through a series of indirect but interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Apomorphine may act as a mediator in the development of this condition, as it is associated with atypical juvenile parkinsonism and x-linked parkinsonism-spasticity syndrome, both of which are linked to the disease. These conditions are characterized by the presence of specific drugs that can influence the progression of Parkinson's disease, suggesting a potential role for Apomorphine in modulating pathological processes. The evidence also highlights the involvement of opicapone and amantadine, which are used in the treatment of Parkinson's disease, further supporting the idea that Apomorphine may interact with these compounds in a manner that could influence the disease's onset. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the integration of these pathways suggests that Apomorphine may contribute to the development of Juvenile Onset Parkinson Disease 19a through a complex interplay of drug-induced effects and underlying pathophysiological processes.",
